  "definicionElectricidad": "Definition of Electricity",
  "origenElectricidad": "The Term Electricity Comes from the Greek Word 'Elektron' (Amber). The Word owes its Origin to the First Materials Used when Observing the Existence of an Electric Force. Currently, it is a Science, a Branch of Physics, that Studies the Electric Charge and its Interaction with Others, as well as its Effect on the Surrounding Environment.",
  "conceptoCarga": "The Current Concept of Electric Charge has been Constructed over the Years from a Series of Discoveries:",
  "efectosAmbar": "600 BC Attractive Effects in Amber Rubbed with Skin - Thales of Miletus",
  "postulacionSustancia": "1600. Postulation of the Existence of a Substance ('Electron') Responsible for this Phenomenon ('Electricity') - William Gilbert",
  "tiposSustancia": "1752. Postulation of the Existence of Two Types of Substance, Positive Electric Charge and Negative Electric Charge (Convention) - Benjamin Franklin",
  "descubrimientoElectron": "1897. Discovery of the Electron in Experiments with Cathode Ray Tubes - Joseph John Thomson",
  "integranDiferenciales": "Both differentials are integrated and each one is added to the constant of the variable that is different from the differential (c(x) or c(y)).",
  "variableConstante": "The variable is taken as a constant when integrating. Then the values of each constant are found by equalizing them to the solution of the Integral of the other differential and removing the terms that are repeated in both solutions",
  "sustituyenIntegral": "Subsequently, each one is substituted in its respective integral and is equated to C to obtain the general solution.",
  "convencionFranklin": "Benjamin Franklin's convention",
  "procesoCarga": "It is a process of charging by friction, the glass rod acquires positive charge after having been rubbed with silk cloth, and the ebonite rod acquires negative charge after having been rubbed with rabbit skin.",
  "dosTiposCarga": "In nature there are two types of electric charge called positive and negative. Charges of the same sign repel each other and charges of opposite signs attract each other.",
  "definicionCarga": "Definition of electric charge",
  "cargaElectrostatica": "The Electric Charge is a property of matter that explains the origin of electrostatic interactions (electric forces).",
  "propiedadCarga": "The Electric Charge is a property that quantifies the gain or loss of electrons.",
  "produccionCarga": "How is electric charge produced?",
  "principioConservacion": "The Principle of Conservation of Electric Charge states that there is no net destruction or creation of electric charge, in every electrical process the total charge of an isolated system is conserved.",
  "transferenciaCarga": "Formally it is not possible to create or destroy electric charge, however it is possible to transfer charge from one body to another through different charging and discharging processes.",
  "procesosTransferencia": "Charge transfer processes",
  "procesosCarga": "1) Charging Processes: Transfer of charge by contact, by rubbing and by induction.",
  "procesosDescarga": "2) Discharging Processes: Ground connection, ionization and electric wind.",
  "clasificacionMateriales": "Classification of materials (electricity)",
  "materialConductor": "Conductor Material: It is any substance with a large number of free charge carriers, it is capable of carrying a large number of them. Example: Metals, ionized gases, electrolytes.",
  "materialDielectrico": "Dielectric Material (Insulator or Semiconductor): It is any substance that does not have free charge carriers or has a very reduced number of them, this substance cannot transport free charge carriers. Example: Plastic, oil, noble gases.",
  "cargaElectron": "Charge of the Electron",
  "cargaProton": "Charge of the Proton",
  "unidadCarga": "Unit of Measure of Electric Charge (SI)",
  "naturalezaCarga": "The Electric Charge Has a Discrete Nature (Integer Number of Electrons) however it is not always presented in the form of a point charge (the dimension of the charge is negligible compared to the physical dimensions of the problem).",
  "distribucionCarga": "Often the Electric Charge is Presented in the Form of a Continuous Distribution of Charge Along a Line, Surface or Volume, Or in Complex Forms like Electron Densities in Atoms or in Molecular Orbitals.",
  "densidadLineal": "Linear Charge Density",
  "densidadSuperficial": "Surface Charge Density",
  "densidadVolumetrica": "Volume Charge Density",
  "leyCoulombTexto": "Coulomb's Law States that between a Pair of Point Charges an Electric Force of Repulsion or Attraction is Exerted, proportional to the Product of the Charges, inversely proportional to the Square of the Separation Distance between Them and that Acts on the Vector that Unites Them.",
  "unidadFuerza": "Unit of Measure of Force (SI)",
  "constanteCoulomb": "Coulomb's Constant",
  "permitividadVacio": "Permittivity of Vacuum",
  "principioSuperposicionTexto": "The Superposition Principle States that in a System of Charges, The Electric Force That is Exerted Between a Pair of Charges Will Not Depend on the Presence or Absence of Additional Charges, Therefore The Total Electric Force on a Charge Can Be Calculated Considering the Contribution Between Pairs of Charges and Subsequently through a Vector Sum.",
  "flujoCampoVectorial": "The Flow of a Vector Field Can Be Understood as a Measure of the Flow or Penetration of the Vectors Associated with the Vector Field Through a Fixed and Imaginary Surface Element",
  "flujoCampoVectorialSuperficie": "The Flow of a Vector Field is a Measure of the Number of Vector Field Lines that Cross the Area A Surface",
